            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Makes an array shorter by taking out the elements at some positions, closing up the gaps.\n\nUse it to drop joints a model does not need, remove a bad frame or a glitched channel, or take the first or last element off a list.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.delete <indices: ints ...>\nDefault: none - the array passes through unchanged.\n\nEXAMPLES:\nnp.delete 0\nnp.delete 0 -1\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe array (a list is turned into one). Every input has the elements taken out and is sent.\n\nindices:\nThe positions to take out, replacing any given as arguments. Held. -1 is the last;\npositions that do not exist are ignored.\n\naxis (int box, also an inlet):\nWhich way: 0 (the default) elements of a list, or rows of a matrix; 1 columns.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nremaining:\nThe shorter array.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\n10 20 30 40 without its first and last elements (positions 0 and -1): 20 30. A metro re-sends the list twice a second.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.insert and np.append put elements in. np.take keeps the positions you name instead of removing them. np.[] and np.crop cut out a run.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 820,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Makes an array shorter by taking out the elements at some positions, closing up the gaps.\n\nUse it to drop joints a model does not need, remove a bad frame or a glitched channel, or take the first or last element off a list.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.delete <indices: ints ...>\nDefault: none - the array passes through unchanged.\n\nEXAMPLES:\nnp.delete 0\nnp.delete 0 -1\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe array (a list is turned into one). Every input has the elements taken out and is sent.\n\nindices:\nThe positions to take out, replacing any given as arguments. Held. -1 is the last;\npositions that do not exist are ignored.\n\naxis (int box, also an inlet):\nWhich way: 0 (the default) elements of a list, or rows of a matrix; 1 columns.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nremaining:\nThe shorter array.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\n10 20 30 40 without its first and last elements (positions 0 and -1): 20 30. A metro re-sends the list twice a second.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.insert and np.append put elements in. np.take keeps the positions you name instead of removing them. np.[] and np.crop cut out a run."
        },
